.TH SCREENSHARING 1 "2026-08-15" "1.0" "sway dotfiles"
.SH NAME
screensharing \- share a single window, or a whole screen, from sway
.SH DESCRIPTION
When an application asks to capture the screen \[em] a video call, a browser
calling
.IR getDisplayMedia() ,
Vesktop going live \[em] it does not talk to sway. It talks to
.B xdg-desktop-portal
over D-Bus, which routes the request to the wlroots backend
.RB ( xdg-desktop-portal-wlr ,
"xdpw"), which asks a helper program \[em] the
.I chooser
\[em] what to capture, and finally hands the application a
.B PipeWire
node carrying the frames.
.PP
sway 1.12 added capture of individual windows, and xdpw 0.8.2 already knew how
to request one. The piece that was missing on this machine was the chooser: the
default is
.BR slurp (1),
which selects a rectangle on screen and therefore can only ever name an
.IR output .
The capability was present and unreachable \[em] nothing was broken, nothing was
missing, and sharing a single window did not work.
.PP
This system replaces that chooser with a
.I dmenu-style
one. In that mode xdpw hands the candidate list to the chooser on standard
input, so windows can be listed, picked, and named back.
.SH KEYBINDINGS
.TP
.B (none)
There is no key to press. The flow is driven entirely by the requesting
application; the chooser appears when an application asks to capture.
.SH USAGE
.nr step 1 1
.IP \n[step]. 3
Start a screen share in the application (Vesktop "Go Live", a browser's share
button, a meeting client).
.IP \n+[step].
A
.BR rofi (1)
list appears, with one entry per candidate:
.RS
.PP
.nf
[pantalla]  eDP-1 Sharp Corporation 0x1453 (eDP-1)
[ventana]   Inbox \- Betterbird
[ventana]   ~/projects/example
.fi
.RE
.IP \n+[step].
Pick one and the share starts. Press
.I Escape
to decline \[em] the application is told the user cancelled.
.PP
Whether windows appear at all is the requesting application's choice: they are
listed only when it asks for window sources. An application that asks for
screens only gets the screen list, unchanged.
.SH FILES
.SS Configuration
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/xdg-desktop-portal-wlr/config
The xdpw backend config. Sets
.I chooser_type=dmenu
so xdpw feeds the candidate list on stdin, and points
.I chooser_cmd
at the chooser below. Read once at xdpw startup.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/xdg-desktop-portal-wlr/chooser.sh
The chooser. Reads the list on stdin, renders a cleaned-up label per line in
.BR rofi (1),
and prints the chosen line back to stdout verbatim.
.SS "Package-shipped routing"
.TP
.I /usr/share/xdg-desktop-portal/portals/wlr.portal
Declares that the wlr backend implements the
.I Screenshot
and
.I ScreenCast
interfaces, and that it is used in desktops named
.IR sway ,
among others.
.TP
.I /usr/share/xdg-desktop-portal/sway-portals.conf
Routing under a sway session: everything defaults to the GTK backend, except
.I ScreenCast
and
.I Screenshot
which go to wlr. This is why screen capture reaches xdpw with no user config at
all.
.SS Units
.TP
.I xdg-desktop-portal-wlr.service
The backend, a
.I static
systemd user unit. Not enabled and not meant to be: it is started on demand
through its D-Bus name.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/services/org.freedesktop.impl.portal.desktop.wlr.service
The D-Bus activation entry that starts the backend the first time something
asks to capture.
.SH FLOW
.nr step 1 1
.IP \n[step]. 3
The application calls
.IR CreateSession ", " SelectSources ", " Start
on
.I org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast
over the session bus.
.IP \n+[step].
.B xdg-desktop-portal
routes those calls to the wlr backend, per
.IR sway-portals.conf .
.IP \n+[step].
xdpw builds the candidate list. Outputs always; toplevels only if the
application asked for window sources.
.IP \n+[step].
xdpw runs
.I chooser.sh
with that list on stdin, one candidate per line:
.RS
.PP
.nf
Monitor: eDP-1 Sharp Corporation 0x1453 (eDP-1)
Window: <window title> (<ext-foreign-toplevel-list-v1 id>)
.fi
.RE
.IP \n+[step].
The chooser shows readable labels in rofi and asks it for the
.I index
of the selection, then prints the original line \[em] ids intact \[em] on
stdout.
.IP \n+[step].
xdpw creates a capture source: an output source, or a foreign-toplevel source
via
.IR ext_foreign_toplevel_image_capture_source_manager_v1 .
.IP \n+[step].
The frames are published as a
.B PipeWire
node whose id is returned to the application, tagged
.I source_type
1 for a screen or 2 for a window.
.SH PROGRAMS
.BR rofi (1),
.BR slurp (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR gdbus (1)
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.B xdg-desktop-portal
The portal frontend on D-Bus. Applications only ever talk to this.
.TP
.B xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
The wlroots backend that implements ScreenCast and Screenshot, runs the chooser
and talks the capture protocols to the compositor. Per-window capture needs
0.8.2 or newer.
.TP
.B swayfx
The compositor. Per-window capture needs a sway 1.12 base or newer, which
SwayFX 0.6 provides.
.TP
.B rofi
Renders the chooser list. Any dmenu-compatible program works; this one is used
with
.I -format i
so the selection comes back as an index.
.TP
.B pipewire
Carries the captured frames from the compositor to the application.
.TP
.B xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
Handles every other portal interface under this session; unrelated to capture,
but removing it breaks file pickers.
.SH GOTCHAS
.IP \[bu] 2
.B The capability was never switched off.
Before any of this config existed, the portal already advertised window capture
to every application on the system:
.RS
.PP
.nf
gdbus call --session --dest org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop \\
  --object-path /org/freedesktop/portal/desktop \\
  --method org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties.Get \\
  org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast AvailableSourceTypes
\[->] (<uint32 3>,)      # 1 = screen, 2 = window, so 3 = both
.fi
.PP
Only the chooser could not name a window. Check this property first before
believing that window capture is unsupported.
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
.B slurp can never answer with a window.
It selects a rectangle, and a rectangle has no identity. Any chooser that
selects geometry is structurally incapable of naming a toplevel, no matter what
the compositor supports.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B chooser_type decides what the chooser is given.
With
.IR simple ", "
the chooser is run with nothing on stdin and must produce a
.I "Monitor: "
or
.I "Window: <id>"
line on its own \[em] which means discovering toplevel ids itself, via
.BR lswt (1),
which is not installed here. With
.IR dmenu ", "
xdpw supplies the list, ids included. That is the practical route to windows.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B The answer must be byte-identical to a line that was offered.
Anything else is read as "declined". This is why the chooser displays a
prettified label but asks rofi for the
.I index
.RI ( -format " i)"
and echoes the untouched original line \[em] rewriting the text to look nicer
would silently cancel every share.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B Empty stdout means the user declined; exit code 127 is reserved.
xdpw uses 127 internally to signal "no such command" and throws away everything
such a chooser printed. A chooser must never exit 127.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B Windows are offered only when the application asks for them.
Measured: a request for source type 1 gets one screen line and zero window
lines; a request for type 3 gets the screen plus every capturable toplevel.
Applications that only ever wanted a monitor behave exactly as they did before.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B The config is read once, at backend startup.
Editing it changes nothing until the backend restarts:
.RS
.PP
.nf
systemctl --user restart xdg-desktop-portal-wlr.service
.fi
.PP
The unit is
.I static
and D-Bus activated \[em] there is nothing to
.IR enable .
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
.B "A file named after the desktop outranks config."
xdpw searches
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xdg-desktop-portal-wlr/$XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
before
.IR .../config .
The backend process runs with
.I XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=sway
even though the compositor's own value is
.IR sway:wlroots:swayfx ,
so a file literally named
.I sway
in that directory would win and this config would be ignored without a word.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B A window stream is the window, not a crop of the screen.
Captured through the portal, a 603x990 window on a 1920x1080 output arrives as a
603x990 stream \[em] no wallpaper, no neighbouring windows, no titlebar. Moving
or covering the window does not change what the viewer sees.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B A still window produces no frames.
Capture is damage-driven: an idle window emits nothing until it redraws. A
consumer waiting for a fixed number of frames will appear to hang on a static
window. That is the compositor being efficient, not the capture failing.
.IP \[bu] 2
.B Sharing a browser tab does not exercise this path.
Chromium and its derivatives capture their own tabs internally, without the
portal. To test the portal chain, share a window or an entire screen.
.SH NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
Nothing here starts at login. The backend is activated by D-Bus the first time
an application asks to capture, and it reads the config at that moment.
.IP \[bu] 2
Both files are tracked in the dotfiles repository; a fresh machine gets this
behaviour as soon as they are checked out and rofi is installed.
.IP \[bu] 2
Distinct from its siblings:
.BR screenshots (1)
captures a still image to disk or clipboard, and
.BR recording (1)
records the screen to a file. This page is about handing live frames to another
application.
